.TH DATE 1
.SH NAME
date \- print and set the date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "date [ \-l ] [ yymmddhhmm [.ss] ]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
If no argument is given, the current date and time are printed.
If an argument is given, the current date is set.
.I yy
is the last two digits of the year; the first
.I mm
is the month number;
.I dd
is the day number in the month;
.I hh
is the hour number (24 hour system); the second
.I mm
is the minute number;
.I .ss
is optional and is the seconds.
The year, month and day may be omitted, the current values being the defaults.
The system operates in GMT.
.B date
takes care of the conversion to and from local standard and daylight time.
.PP
The
.B \-l
flag causes the current date to be loaded from the DEC Professional battery backed-up clock (PRO/VENIX only).
This clock maintains the correct date even when the hardware is turned off, so that the system administrator need not set the date himself.
Normally,
.B `date \-l'
is executed from the
.B /etc/rc
file on boot-up.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set the date to Oct 8, 12:45 AM.
.RS
.nf
date 10080045
.fi
.RE
Print today's date:
.RS
.nf
date
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP 20
.B /usr/adm/wtmp
to record time-setting
.SH "SEE ALSO"
utmp(4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`No permission' if you aren't the super-user and you try to change the date; `bad conversion' if the date set is syntactically incorrect.
The
.B \-l
flag will produce an error if the battery backed-up clock is unreliable (usually due to power being off for a prolonged period), in which case the date must be set manually.
.sp
